: \nL257:     cite23†Seminars & Events cite24†Seminars List cite25†Events List L258: \nL259: PhD Program\nL260: \nL261:     cite26†PhD Program Top cite27†Admissions cite28†For Students L262: \nL263: Collaborative Research\nL264: \nL265:     cite29†Outline cite30†BioResource cite31†Databases cite32†IBBP cite44†Cooperation in Japan L266: \nL267: International Cooperation\nL268:     cite33†Outline cite34†International Activities cite35†NIBB Conferences cite36†Practical Courses cite37†COS cite38†EMBL cite40†Princeton University cite39†TLL cite41†MPIPZ cite42†Okazaki Biology Conferences L269: \nL270: Nishigonaka 38, Myodaiji, Okazaki 444-8585 Aichi, Japan\nL271: \nL272: Copyright© 2011-2026 National Institute for Basic\nL273: Biology. All rights reserved.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExploding Lakes in Cameroon | U.S. Geological Survey (https://www.usgs.gov/media/images/exploding-lakes-cameroon-2)\nciteturn10view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.usgs.gov/media/images/exploding-lakes-cameroon-2\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 39\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLake Nyos, Cameroon - NASA Science (https://science.nasa.gov/earth-science/programs/research-analysis/lake-nyos-cameroon/)\nciteturn10view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth-science/programs/research-analysis/lake-nyos-cameroon/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 342\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCameroon's Lake Nyos Gas Burst: 30 Years Later - Eos (https://doi.org/10.1029/2016eo055627)\nciteturn10view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doi.org/10.1029/2016eo055627\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://eos.org/science-updates/cameroons-lake-nyos-gas-burst-30-years-later; Total lines: 151\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFailure to detect changes to people during a real-world interaction (https://gwern.net/doc/psychology/cognitive-bias/illusion-of-depth/1998-simons.pdf)\nciteturn10view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 6; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view4\",\"lineno\":90}); Total lines: 382\nL39@P0: 820 William James Hall, 33 Kirkland St., Cambridge, MA 02138 (e\u0002mail: dsimons@wjh.harvard.edu) or D. T. Levin, Department of Psy\u0002chology, Kent State University, P.O. Box 5190, Kent, OH 44242-0001\nL40@P0: (e-mail: dlevin@kent.edu).\nL41@P0: BRIEF REPORTS\nL42@P0: Failure to detect changes to people\nL43@P0: during a real-world interaction\nL44@P0: DANIEL J. SIMONS\nL45@P0: Harvard University, Cambridge, Massachusetts\nL46@P0: and\nL47@P0: DANIEL T. LEVIN\nL48@P0: Kent State University, Kent, Ohio\nL49@P0: Recent research on change detection has documented surprising failures to detect visual changes oc\u0002curring between views of a scene, suggesting the possibility that visual representations contain few de\u0002tails. Although these studies convincingly demonstrate change blindness for objects in still images and\nL50@P0: motion pictures, they may not adequately assess the capacity to represent objects in the real world.\nL51@P0: Here we examine and reject the possibility that change blindness in previous studies resulted from pas\u0002sive viewing of 2-D displays. In one experiment, an experimenter initiated a conversation with a pedes\u0002trian, and during the interaction, he was surreptitiously replaced by a different experimenter. Only half\nL52@P0: of the pedestrians detected the change. Furthermore, successful detection depended on social group\nL53@P0: membership; pedestrians from the same social group as the experimenters detected the change but\nL54@P0: those from a different social group did not. A second experiment further examined the importance of\nL55@P0: this effect of social group. Provided that the meaning of the scene is unchanged, changes to attended\nL56@P0-1: objects can escape detection even when they occur during a natural, real-world interaction. The dis\u0002cussion provides a set of guidelines and suggestions for future research on change blindness.CHANGE DETECTION 645\nL57@P1: A recent series of studies directly examined the role of\nL58@P1: attention in the detection of changes to natural images\nL59@P1: (Rensink et al., 1997). In their “flicker paradigm,” an orig\u0002inal version and a modified version of an image were pre\u0002sented in rapid alternation (240 msec each), with a blank\nL60@P1: screen (80-msec duration) interposed between them, pro\u0002ducing a flickering appearance. On each trial, subjects\nL61@P1: were asked to identify the changing part of the image as\nL62@P1: soon as they saw it. Consistent with earlier studies of in\u0002tegration across views (for a review, see Irwin, 1991), ob\u0002servers rarely noticed changes during the first cycle of\nL63@P1: alternation and often required many cycles to detect the\nL64@P1: change. The change detection process requires observers\nL65@P1: to shift their attention among the objects in the scene, ac\u0002tively searching for a change. As predicted by models of\nL66@P1: object files, changes to objects that independent raters\nL67@P1: consider to be the center of interest of a scene are de\u0002tected in significantly fewer alternations than changes to\nL68@P1: peripheral objects. That is, changes to the details of at\u0002tended objects are detected more readily.\nL69@P1: Clearly, focused attention to an object is helpful and\nL70@P1: possibly necessary for change detection, as evidenced by\nL71@P1: such “center of interest” effects (O’Regan, Rensink, &\nL72@P1: Clark, 1996; Rensink et al., 1997; Tarr & Aginsky, 1996,\nL73@P1: July) and by findings of more successful change detection\nL74@P1: when explicit cues specify the location or the type of\nL75@P1: change (Aginsky, Tarr, & Rensink, 1997). However, atten\u0002tion may not be sufficient for change detection. In fact,\nL76@P1: observers often fail to detect changes even when atten\u0002tion is focused directly on the changing object (Levin &\nL77@P1: Simons, 1997; O’Regan et al., 1997; Simons, 1996). In a\nL78@P1: recent series of studies, we used motion pictures to directly\nL79@P1: examine the ability to detect changes to attended objects\nL80@P1: (Levin & Simons, 1997). These brief motion pictures de\u0002picted a simple action performed by a “single” actor. Dur\u0002ing the film, the actor was replaced by a different person.\nL81@P1: For example, in one film an actor walked through an\nL82@P1: empty classroom and began to sit in a chair. The camera\nL83@P1: then changed, or “cut,” to a closer view and a different\nL84@P1: actor completed the action. Even though the actors were\nL85@P1: easily discriminable and were the focus of attention, only\nL86@P1: 33% of the 40 participants reported noticing the change\nL87@P1: from one actor to another (Levin & Simons, 1997).\nL88@P1: Although the motion picture experiments demonstrate\nL89@P1: that attention alone is not sufficient for a complete rep\u0002resentation of the visual details of an object, they do not\nL90@P1: fully assess our ability to represent objects in the real\nL91@P1: world. Motion picture perception is similar in many ways\nL92@P1: to perception in the real world, but motion pictures are\nL93@P1: still a subset of a complete visual experience (Arnheim,\nL94@P1: 1933/1966). Most importantly, they are viewed passively\nL95@P1: and may not completely engage the processes necessary\nL96@P1: for a complete representation of attended objects. Fur\u0002thermore, cuts from one view to another in motion pictures\nL97@P1: may artificially hamper our ability to detect changes. Al\u0002though cuts are similar in some ways to eye movements,\nL98@P1: they also instantaneously change the simulated observa\u0002tion point. This artificial jump in viewing position may\nL99@P1: somehow disrupt the ability to detect changes even if it\nL100@P1: has little effect on our understanding of a scene. Similar\nL101@P1: objections might be raised about most studies document\u0002ing change blindness (for a discussion, see Simons &\nL102@P1: Levin, 1997). In all previous studies of change blindness,\nL103@P1: exposure to scenes has been mediated via photographs,\nL104@P1: computer displays, or television monitors. Perhaps peo\u0002ple can more fully represent the details of a scene when\nL105@P1: they are direct participants, interacting with the objects\nL106@P1: in the real world.\nL107@P1: Here we assess this possibility by taking the study of\nL108@P1: change blindness into the real world. Rather than changing\nL109@P1: the sole actor in a video, we changed the subjects’ con\u0002versation partner during a typical daily interaction.\nL110@P1: EXPERIMENT 1\nL111@P1: In Experiment 1, we created a situation that allowed us\nL112@P1: to surreptitiously substitute one individual for another in\nL113@P1: the middle of a natural, real-world interaction. The situ\u0002ation we chose was asking directions of a pedestrian on\nL114@P1: a college campus.1 We temporarily interrupted this inter\u0002action by carrying a door between the experimenter and\nL115@P1: the pedestrian. While the experimenter was occluded by\nL116@P1: the door, another experimenter took his place and con\u0002tinued the interaction after the door had passed. If change\u0002detection failures are based on the passive nature of me\u0002diated stimuli, these substitutions should be clearly\nL117@P1: detectable.\nL118@P1: Method\nL119@P1: Subjects. A total of 15 pedestrians were approached on the campus\nL120@P1: of Cornell University. They ranged in approximate age from 20 to 65.\nL121@P1: Only pedestrians walking alone or together with one other person (two\nL122@P1: cases) were approached.\nL123@P1: Procedure. An experimenter carrying a campus map asked unsus\u0002pecting pedestrians for directions to a nearby building (see Figure 1a).\nL124@P1: Pedestrians had a clear view of the experimenter starting from a dis\u0002tance of approximately 20 m as they walked down a sidewalk. After the\nL125@P1: experimenter and pedestrian had been talking for 10–15 sec, two other\nL126@P1: experimenters carrying a door rudely passed between them. As the door\nL127@P1: passed, the first experimenter grabbed the back of the door, and the ex\u0002perimenter who had been carrying that part of the door stayed behind\nL128@P1: and continued to ask for directions (Figure 1c). The first experimenter\nL129@P1: kept his map during the interruption, and the second experimenter pro\u0002duced an identical copy of the map after the door passed. The door\nL130@P1: blocked the pedestrian’s view for approximately 1 sec (Figure 1b). From\nL131@P1: the subject’s perspective, the door briefly occluded his/her conversation\nL132@P1: partner, and when it was gone, a different person was revealed. As the\nL133@P1: door passed, subjects typically made eye contact with the second ex\u0002perimenter before continuing to give directions.2 The entire interaction\nL134@P1: took 2–5 min. The two experimenters wore different clothing and dif\u0002fered in height by approximately 5 cm (Figure 1d). Their voices were\nL135@P1: also clearly distinguishable.\nL136@P1: After a pedestrian finished giving directions, the experimenter told\nL137@P1: him/her, “We’re doing a study as part of the psychology department [ex\u0002perimenter points to the psychology building next door] of the sorts of\nL138@P1: things people pay attention to in the real world. Did you notice anything\nL139@P1-2: unusual at all when that door passed by a minute ago?” Responses were646 SIMONS AND LEVIN\nL140@P2: noted by the experimenter, and if subjects failed to report the change,\nL141@P2: they were directly asked, “Did you notice that I’m not the same person\nL142@P2: who approached you to ask for directions?” After answering this ques\u0002tion, all subjects were informed about the purpose of the experiment.\nL143@P2: Results and Discussion\nL144@P2: If change blindness results from the passive nature of\nL145@P2: mediated stimuli, then these real-world substitutions\nL146@P2: should be detected. When asked if they had noticed any\u0002thing unusual, most pedestrians reported that the people\nL147@P2: carrying the door were rude. Yet, despite clear differences\nL148@P2: in clothing, appearance, and voice, only 7 of the 15 pedes\u0002trians reported noticing the change of experimenters.\nL149@P2: Those who did not notice the change continued the con\u0002versation as if nothing had happened (in fact, some\nL150@P2: pedestrians who did notice the change also continued the\nL151@P2: conversation!). Pedestrians who did not notice the change\nL152@P2: were quite surprised to learn that the person standing in\nL153@P2: front of them was different from the one who initiated\nL154@P2: the conversation. One pedestrian who reported noticing\nL155@P2: nothing unusual nonetheless claimed to have noticed the\nL156@P2: change when asked directly.\nL157@P2: Interestingly, those who noticed the change were all\nL158@P2: students of roughly the same age as the experimenters\nL159@P2: (approximately 20–30 years old). Those who failed to\nL160@P2: detect the change were slightly older than the experi\u0002menters (approximately 35–65 years old). One possible\nL161@P2: explanation for this difference is that younger pedestri\u0002ans were more likely to expend effort encoding those\nL162@P2: features that would differentiate the experimenters be\u0002cause the experimenters were roughly of their own gen\u0002eration. In contrast, older pedestrians would likely en\u0002code the experimenters without focusing on features that\nL163@P2: could differentiate the two of them, instead viewing them\nL164@P2: as members of a social group other than their own. This\nL165@P2: hypothesis draws on findings from social psychology\nL166@P2: that members of one’s own social group (“in-group”) are\nL167@P2: treated differently from members of social groups dis\u0002tinctly apart from one’s own (“out-group”). Upon encoun\u0002tering a member of an in-group, people tend to focus at\u0002Figure 1. Frames from a video of a subject from Experiment 1. Frames a–c show the sequence of the switch. Frame d shows the two\nL168@P2-3: experimenters side by side.CHANGE DETECTION 647\nL169@P3: tention on individuating features and to pay little attention\nL170@P3: to the person’s social-group membership. In contrast, for\nL171@P3: members of out-groups, people direct more attention to\nL172@P3: attributes associated with the out-group as a whole and\nL173@P3: generally do not focus on features that distinguish one in\u0002dividual from others in the group (see, e.g., Rothbart &\nL174@P3: John, 1985). These differences in processing of members\nL175@P3: of in-groups and out-groups extend to many aspects of\nL176@P3: cognition. For example, people are likely to assume that\nL177@P3: members of out-groups are collectively less variable on a\nL178@P3: variety of traits and variables (Judd & Park, 1988; Linville,\nL179@P3: Fischer, & Salovey, 1989). This tendency to code group\u0002specifying information for members of out-groups can\nL180@P3: even determine what represents a visual feature for a par\u0002ticular category (Levin, 1996).\nL181@P3: Applying these differences in the coding of in-groups\nL182@P3: and out-groups to the findings of Experiment 1, we hy\u0002pothesize that the younger subjects considered them\u0002selves members of the same social group as the experi\u0002menters and older subjects considered the experimenters\nL183@P3: to be members of an out-group. To test this hypothesis,\nL184@P3: we changed the appearance of the experimenters so that\nL185@P3: they could be classified as members of an out-group by\nL186@P3: the younger subjects.\nL187@P3: EXPERIMENT 2\nL188@P3: To examine the role of social group membership in the\nL189@P3: detection of changes, a second experiment was conducted\nL190@P3: using the same procedure as the first, but with one criti\u0002cal change: The same two experimenters dressed as con\u0002struction workers (see Figure 2). The experimenters again\nL191@P3: wore different clothing: One wore a construction hat with\nL192@P3: writing on the front, a large tool belt, and a light blue shirt,\nL193@P3: and the other wore a newer hat without writing, no tool\nL194@P3: belt, and a black shirt. The experiment was conducted in\nL195@P3: the same location as Experiment 1, which happened to be\nL196@P3: approximately 50 m from a construction site. As in Ex\u0002periment 1, an experimenter approached a pedestrian to\nL197@P3: ask for directions to a building on campus. During the\nL198@P3: conversation, the experimenters were switched. Unlike\nL199@P3: in the first experiment, all 12 pedestrians who partici\u0002pated in Experiment 2 were from the younger age group\nL200@P3: (Cornell graduate or undergraduate students), the group\nL201@P3: that had always detected the change in Experiment 1.\nL202@P3: The questions asked of the subjects were identical to those\nL203@P3: of Experiment 1 except that subjects were informed im\u0002mediately after providing directions that the experimenters\nL204@P3: were not actually construction workers but were doing a\n"
